{0 Temporal Phase Semantics}

This note fixes the vocabulary used by the temporal proof generator. It is a
design note, not a new proof strategy.

{1 End-of-instant product states}

A product state is an end-of-instant state:

- the program component is the control state after the program step has run;
- the assumption automaton component is the automaton state after reading the
  instant trace;
- the guarantee automaton component is the automaton state after reading the
  instant trace.

Thus, at instant [n], the product state [(s_n, a_n, g_n)] denotes the state
after instant [n] has completed. The previous product state
[(s_{n-1}, a_{n-1}, g_{n-1})] is the state after instant [n-1] has completed.

{1 Step context}

A program transition from [s_{n-1}] to [s_n] is selected and executed during
instant [n]. Its guard belongs to the step context of instant [n]. It is not a
fact about the end of instant [n-1], and it is not a fact that can be shifted
to instant [n+1] with [pre].

The same distinction applies to assumption-side guards used to select the
current product edge. They constrain the current step. They are not persistent
facts about the next product source state unless a separate semantic rule
proves that persistence.

{1 Meaning of [pre]}

The expression [pre(x)] at instant [n] denotes the value of [x] at the end of
instant [n-1]. More generally, [pre^k(x)] denotes an earlier completed
instant. It never denotes an internal "before executing the previous step"
phase.

Consequently, replacing a step-context fact by [pre] or [pre^2] does not
recover the phase in which a transition guard was evaluated. If the step body
can update a variable, then the value observed by [pre] is the post-step value
of the previous instant, not the pre-body value used by the previous guard.

{1 Current trace point}

The automata for assumptions and guarantees read the current trace point.
Their edges are part of the product step from
[(s_{n-1}, a_{n-1}, g_{n-1})] to [(s_n, a_n, g_n)]. The guarantee edge guard
is therefore a current-step formula: it must be justified from the current
execution and the source product state.

{1 Soundness boundaries}

These boundaries must hold in the generator and in the Rocq development:

- product exploration must not remove an edge because a syntactic conjunction
  mixes source facts and destination facts that look inconsistent;
- the proof generator must not turn a program guard or assumption guard from a
  step-context fact into a previous-tick hypothesis for a later instant;
- reachability of a product state must be represented either by an explicit
  product-reachability argument or by a certified invariant, not by an
  unproved reconstruction from shifted guards;
- for one source precondition, possible guarantee-edge outcomes must remain
  grouped as a disjunction when proving that the program step forces at least
  one safe product successor.

{1 Intended relation}

The intended semantic object is a step relation of the following shape:

{[
reachable_product_state(n - 1, src)
and selected_program_transition(n, tr)
and selected_assumption_edge(n, ae)
and selected_guarantee_edge(n, ge)
and executes_to(n, tr, src.program_state, dst.program_state)
implies reachable_product_state(n, dst)
]}

The important point is that [selected_program_transition],
[selected_assumption_edge], and [selected_guarantee_edge] are facts of instant
[n]. They are not reconstructed at instant [n+1] by applying [pre] to their
guards.

{1 Product Reachability}

There are two different notions of reachability in the implementation, and
they must not be confused.

[Product_build] computes graph reachability: a product state is discovered if
there is a path in the syntactic product graph from the initial product state.
This exploration deliberately follows every local combination of program
transition, assumption edge, and guarantee edge. It does not prove that the
guards of the path can hold together along a concrete execution.

Semantic product reachability is stronger. A product state [p] is semantically
reachable at end of instant [n] when there exists a concrete execution prefix
of length [n] such that:

- the program component of [p] is the control state obtained after executing
  the program step of instant [n];
- the assumption component of [p] is the state obtained by feeding the same
  instant trace to the assumption automaton;
- the guarantee component of [p] is the state obtained by feeding the same
  instant trace to the guarantee automaton;
- for every previous instant in the prefix, the program transition guard,
  assumption edge guard, and guarantee edge guard used by the product step
  were true in the correct phase of that instant.

For proof obligations about guarantees, the useful source predicate is the
live version of this relation. [LiveReach(n, p)] means that [p] is
semantically reachable at end of instant [n] and that the prefix has not
already reached the bad assumption state or the bad guarantee state. A path
that has reached the bad assumption state is outside the guarantee theorem; a
path that reaches the bad guarantee state is precisely what the safety
obligations must rule out.

The inductive rules are:

- [LiveReach(0, p0)] for the initial product state [p0];
- if [LiveReach(n - 1, src)] holds, the program step from [src.program_state]
  to [dst.program_state] executes at instant [n], the selected assumption edge
  does not go to the bad assumption state, the selected guarantee edge does
  not go to the bad guarantee state, and all three selected guards hold in the
  current step context, then [LiveReach(n, dst)] holds;
- if [LiveReach(n - 1, src)] holds and a selected assumption edge goes to the
  bad assumption state, no live successor is produced;
- if [LiveReach(n - 1, src)] holds and a selected guarantee edge goes to the
  bad guarantee state while the assumption edge is still non-bad, the generated
  safety obligation must prove that this step context is impossible.

The generator may use [LiveReach(n - 1, src)] only as an induction hypothesis
for a source product state of the current product step. It must not manufacture
[LiveReach] from shifted program guards, shifted assumption guards, or local
syntactic compatibility of incoming edges.

{1 Backend Representation}

The current Why3 backend cannot use a bare uninterpreted fact [LiveReach(p)]
as a useful hypothesis. Such a fact would either prove nothing about program
variables or, if asserted without an induction rule, would be an unsound
assumption.

The representation intended for the backend is therefore a finite certified
reachability invariant. For each live product state [p], the generator may
carry a first-order formula [R_p] over end-of-instant values. The formulas
must satisfy the following proof obligations:

- initialization: the initial program-state facts imply [R_p0];
- preservation: for every safe product step [src -> dst], [R_src] together
  with the previous program state, previous guarantee state, current
  step-context program guard, current step-context assumption guard, current
  guarantee edge guard, and the program body implies [R_dst];
- use: every generated obligation for a product step out of [src] may assume
  [R_src] at the previous end-of-instant boundary;
- bad-guarantee exclusion: for every bad-guarantee step out of [src],
  [R_src] and the same step-context facts must imply contradiction.

Soundness requires only that [R_p] over-approximates [LiveReach(_, p)]. A weak
choice such as [R_p = true] is sound but useless. A useful choice may set
[R_p] to [false] only when the preservation proof establishes that no concrete
safe prefix reaches [p]. This is different from dropping product edges: the
edge remains in the explored product, but its obligations are guarded by the
certified source invariant.

This is the precise sense in which product reachability can help with states
such as [(Idle, A0, G1)]. It is not enough to notice locally that the state
has incoming edges; the invariant must record enough end-of-instant
information to prove whether a concrete safe prefix can really end there.
